Dear all,

unfortunately, the solution to our spatial occupancy modeling problem wasn't that simple as a mere scaling problem.

We now scaled the distance matrix and coordinates in the data list in the same kilometric units. Next, we made the bounds of the uniform prior on phi narrower compared to the 'default' of 'c(3/max.dist, 3/min.dist)' (which would be c(~0, 21000)) and used c(0.01, 1000) instead. With 100k iterations and a burnin of 10k, the intercepts-only spatial model with 15 NNGP yields these results,

Occurrence (logit scale):
              Mean     SD    2.5%     50%   97.5%   Rhat ESS

(Intercept) -2.269 0.9557 -4.3608 -1.9742 -0.5972 1.4351  39

Detection (logit scale):
               Mean     SD    2.5%     50%   97.5%   Rhat  ESS

(Intercept) -0.5459 0.1048 -0.7559 -0.5454 -0.3435 1.0035 3072

Spatial Covariance:
             Mean       SD   2.5%     50%    97.5%   Rhat ESS

sigma.sq  12.4414  12.5916 0.2088 11.3352  43.5252 2.9044  17
phi      213.8837 313.1434 0.0137  0.0420 941.7355 2.7849   9,

and abysmal traceplots for the spatial params:

Does anyone have any other suggestions than trying to see what happens when we run the model for 1 or 2 M iterations ?

